      "keyInsight": "Most people assume depression is just extreme sadness. That is like confusing an empty gas tank with an engine explosion. Sadness is an active, living emotion with sharp edges. Depression is the complete absence of feeling, energy, and vitality. Think of an oak tree in winter that forgot how to grow leaves. It is not that you feel terrible pain every second. Rather, your brain turns down the master volume on reality itself. Joy vanishes first, but curiosity and anger pack up and leave too. Even making a slice of toast feels like climbing Mount Everest backwards. The grand paradox is that depression does not come from feeling too much. It happens when your inner fuse box trips to protect you from overload. By cutting off all circuits, it locks you in quiet, cold darkness.",
      "content": "Imagine your home electrical panel during a sudden summer storm. If lightning strikes the power lines, your circuit breakers trip instantly. They shut off the lights to keep the house from burning down. Depression is your nervous system doing the exact same defensive move. It pulls the master breaker on your daily emotional grid. At first, you barely notice the dimmer switch sliding down. Food loses its rich seasoning, music sounds like flat static, and colors turn gray. You tell yourself you just need a solid weekend of sleep. But sleep does not recharge a battery with corroded terminals.\n\nSoon, ordinary daily tasks transform into absurd physical struggles. Turning over in bed feels like swimming through cold, wet cement. You look at your phone buzzing on the bedside table. Picking it up feels as daunting as running an Olympic marathon barefoot. Solomon describes this shift as the gradual loss of everyday vitality. When you are healthy, your mind generates free momentum automatically. You put on your shoes without giving a single lecture to your feet. In deep depression, every tiny muscle twitch requires a separate manual command.\n\nHealthy sadness is actually a sign of working life. When you grieve, you cry, you scream, and you remember love. Your emotional engine is revving hot, burning through fuel to process pain. Depression is what happens when the fuel line completely freezes over. Nothing catches fire, nothing moves, and no smoke comes out. It is not a stormy ocean with crashing waves. It is a dry lakebed where the water quietly vanished weeks ago.",
