      "keyInsight": "Nobody snaps like a dry twig in a single instant. A breakdown is never a surprise thunderbolt from a sunny blue sky. It is more like termites quietly chewing through wooden house beams for years. On the outside, your front porch looks sturdy and nicely painted. You show up for work, chat with neighbors, and water your lawn. But under the floorboards, every support beam is turning into dust. A tiny tap, like a lost set of car keys, finally collapses the roof. People point at the missing keys and blame them for the fall. But the keys were just the final feather on a bent crane. The breakdown actually started thousands of small stresses ago, when nobody was looking.",
      "content": "Think about driving a car down the highway with a cracked radiator. Coolant leaks out drop by drop over months of highway driving. The dashboard temperature gauge creeps up, but you ignore the tiny needle. You crank up the stereo and keep your foot pressed to the gas. Then one morning, you start the car and thick white smoke pours out. The engine block has warped into useless scrap metal. A mental breakdown follows the exact same mechanical pattern of neglected wear.\n\nAndrew Solomon charts his own collapse as a slow, creeping erosion. At first, you patch over your rising anxiety with raw willpower. You drink another cup of dark coffee and force a polite smile. You tell yourself that everyone feels exhausted and frayed at the edges. But behind the curtain, your nervous system is burning through its emergency reserves. Stress hormones marinate your brain tissues without pause or nightly repair. Your internal shock absorbers flatten out like worn rubber tires on gravel.\n\nWhen the system finally gives way, ordinary reality snaps in half. Solomon recalls lying on his bed, unable to dial the phone for help. His tongue felt too heavy, and simple thoughts dissolved like tissue paper. The collapse feels sudden only because our culture ignores hidden cracks. We wait for the bridge to tumble into the river before inspecting the bolts. Recognizing the slow leak early is the only real way to save the engine.",
